    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic coronary disease with stable exertional angina (I25.118)</content>. The presenting exertional chest tightness now has its cause. Coronary CT angiography this morning shows an 80% stenosis of the proximal left anterior descending artery with a CT-derived fractional flow reserve of 0.70, a 30% plaque in the right coronary artery, and a coronary calcium score of 210. The echocardiogram is normal, with LVEF 58%, normal wall motion and valves, and no interatrial shunt on the agitated-saline study. Angina is less frequent on metoprolol 50 mg but still limits him (Canadian Cardiovascular Society class II). Dr. Solberg-Adebayo recommends a higher beta-blocker dose first and revascularization if angina persists. [S]<list><item>Metoprolol succinate up to 100 mg daily.</item><item>Continue aspirin, atorvastatin 80 mg, and nitroglycerin as needed.</item><item>Review in four weeks: if angina persists, discuss coronary angiography and a stent.</item><item>Lipid panel at ten weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nicotine dependence, cigarettes (F17.210)</content>. Not smoked for a week since his quit date; nausea on varenicline is mild. [S]<list><item>Take varenicline with food and a full glass of water.</item><item>Counseling call each week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure (target below 130/80 mmHg)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>134/84 mmHg, above target; expected to fall with the higher metoprolol dose</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, BMI, and physical activity</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>85.6 kg, BMI 28.0; no regular exercise yet</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Angina frequency and Canadian Cardiovascular Society class, nitroglycerin use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two or three episodes a week (class II); nitroglycerin twice</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Coronary CT angiography with CT-FFR as the first test for suspected obstructive coronary disease</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>80% proximal LAD stenosis with CT-FFR 0.70; 30% RCA plaque; calcium score 210</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram at diagnosis</td><td>Once at diagnosis, then only with a change in status</td><td>Completed today</td><td>LVEF 58%; normal wall motion and valves; no shunt</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence review: aspirin (or dual antiplatelet therapy), high-intensity statin, beta-blocker for angina</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Aspirin, atorvastatin, and metoprolol taken daily; metoprolol raised to 100 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing lipid therapy, then every 3 to 12 months</td><td>Due</td><td>Atorvastatin 80 mg started two weeks ago; lipid panel at ten weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HbA1c for prediabetes</td><td>Every 3 to 6 months while lifestyle change is under way, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>5.9% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tobacco use and cessation support (varenicline with counseling)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Abstinent since the quit date a week ago; mild nausea on varenicline</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol use (AUDIT-C) and brief intervention</td><td>At intake, then each visit until within limits</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AUDIT 12 at intake with a brief intervention; now two drinks on most evenings</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-2, then PHQ-9 if positive)</td><td>Periodically</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-2 1 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cardiology consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Solberg-Adebayo today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cardiac rehabilitation referral</td><td>At diagnosis of stable angina, and after PCI</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Recommended by cardiology today but not placed; the referral was held until the decision about revascularization. The protocol calls for a referral at the diagnosis of stable angina</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Remote expedition plan (exercise test, medicines, defibrillator, evacuation, cold stress)</td><td>Before departure</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Pre-departure exercise test and expedition plan agreed with cardiology</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No cigarettes from the quit date, confirmed at each visit. In progress; target 1921-09-08 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol below 55 mg/dL with at least a 50% reduction by four months. In progress; target 1921-09-08 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg by six weeks. In progress; target 1921-06-23 [S]</item><item>Free of angina in daily activity, and a symptom-limited exercise test of at least 10 METs without ischemia before departure. In progress; target 1921-09-08 [S]</item><item>Drinking within the low-risk limit, with an AUDIT-C below 4, by four months. In progress; target 1921-09-08 [S]</item><item>At least 15 sessions of cardiac rehabilitation before departure. In progress; target 1921-09-08 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2023 AHA/ACC/ACCP/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (Virani SS, et al. Circulation 2023;148(9):e9-e119) (2023); 2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of chronic coronary syndromes (Vrints C, et al. Eur Heart J 2024;45(36):3415-3537) (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, ischemic heart disease and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in four weeks. Call emergency services for chest pain that lasts more than 5 minutes after one nitroglycerin, or pain at rest. Next scheduled visit 1921-06-23: six-week review: angina on medical therapy, smoking, and a shared decision about coronary angiography and stenting. [S]</paragraph></text>

